  -->
  <xs:element name="RFIReply" type="RFIReplyType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A reply to an issue within an RFI.  Issues and replies have identical fields except that an issue has the field RFIReply.  See RFILineBaseType for details.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
  </xs:element>
  <xs:complexType name="RFIReplyType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>A reply to an issue within an RFI.  Issues and replies have identical fields except that an issue has the field RFIReply.  See RFILineBaseType for details.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:complexContent>
      <xs:extension base="RFILineBaseType">
        <xs:sequence>
          <xs:element name="Extension" type="RFIReplyExtensionType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Allows additional information not captured in the agcXML specification.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>To build an extension, define a new XML schema in a separate XML namespace.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>To use an extension, import your new  schema into the XML instance for a BOD and add fields to an Extension field. Extension fields can be found in many nouns, headers, components and fields.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
        </xs:sequence>
      </xs:extension>
    </xs:complexContent>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!--
    RFILineBaseType : platform:StatusLineBaseType

    RFILineBaseType : platform:StatusLineBaseType                          | Usage
    .......................................................................|......................................................................
        Title                : platform:DescriptionType  [0..1]            | title for reporting
        Due                  : DateTimeType              [0..1]            | when an issue or reply is due
        AssignedTo           : ParticipantRefType        [0..*]            | who is managing an issue ore authoring a reply
        DependsOn            : RFILineRefType            [0..*]            | sub-issue or sub-reply
        DescriptionAttachment: platform:AttachmentType   [0..*]            | issue or reply as an attachment
        Stage                : WorkflowStageType         [0..*]            | processing stage for this issue or reply such as Started or Complete
        IssuedBy             : ParticipantRefType        [0..1]            | who issued the line item
        IssuedOn             : DateTimeType              [0..1]            | when the issue or reply was issued
        CreatedBy            : ParticipantRefType        [0..1]            | who created the record for this issue or reply
        CreatedOn            : DateTimeType              [0..1]            | when this issue or reply was added to the RFI
        ElapsedTime          : WorkflowElapsedTimeType   [0..1]            | elapsed time in the workflow
        ImportanceCode       : RFIImportanceCodeType     [0..1]            | importance of the RFI such as Medium or High
        DisciplineCode       : DisciplineCodeType        [0..*]            | discipline addressed such as architectural
        CategoryCode         : RFICategoryCodeType       [0..*]            | category for RFI such as TODO
        ImpactAssessment     : ImpactAssessmentType      [0..*]            | project impact for a workflow change
        Failure              : platform:FailureType      [0..*]            | errors logged while handling this issue or reply

    platform:StatusLineBaseType                                            | Usage
    .......................................................................|......................................................................
        platform:LineNumberID     : platform:OpenIDType             [0..1] | line number of this RFI issue or reply
        platform:LineIDSet        : platform:IDSetType              [0..*] | composite line number, if needed
        platform:Description      : platform:DescriptionType        [0..*] | issue or reply in plain text
        platform:Note             : platform:NoteType               [0..*] | discussion among RFI participants on this issue or reply
        platform:DocumentReference: platform:DocumentReferenceType  [0..*] | references to external documents for this issue or reply
        platform:Attachment       : platform:AttachmentType         [0..*] | inline attachment of documents for this issue or reply
        platform:Status           : platform:StatusType             [0..*] | 
  -->
  <xs:complexType name="RFILineBaseType">
    <xs:annotation>
      <xs:documentation>Common fields for issues and replies within an RFI including all context such as attachments, references, dialog and dependencies on replies from sub-issues or sub-replies.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Both an issue and a reply are generically referred to as line items.  An RFI is a line item based document which means a simple layout where all data appear within a line of some kind.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Two lines represent the simplest scenario of a single reply to a single issue.  Though, they can also be related to build a simple or sophisticated graph of sub-issues, sub-replies, independent work streams and sequential work streams.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>For example, a top level issue can split into sub-issues with a reply for each sub-issue and a top level reply built from the sub-replies.  Sub-issues and sub-replies are separate work streams and can be at different workflow stages.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>While this example reads like a simple hierarchy, that can be too strict.  Consider a common structural sub-issue that must be handled before other sub-replies for electrical and plumbing can proceed.  More generally, lines can be related with a directed, acyclic graph (DAG) to express the non-hierarchical workflow dependencies.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Multiple replies for the same issue handle two distinct cases.  In one case, there can be multiple, independent replies gathered by the RFI requestor.  In another case, there can be chained replies such as when a construction manager uses the reply from a plumbing engineer to form a final reply.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Data fields are the same for both line items but the interpretation is different in each case.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Generally, an issue's data fields frame the statement or pose a question.  Supporting data such as cost impact are suggestions, recommendations, constraints or best guesses.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>Generally, a reply's data fields state the expert opinion so that supporting data such as cost impact is more definitive.</xs:documentation>
      <xs:documentation>To see the duality of data in issues and replies, consider that a reply is an expert opinion rather than a statement of fact.  Data in the RFI's requestor's statement of the issue might still prevail.</xs:documentation>
    </xs:annotation>
    <xs:complexContent>
      <xs:extension base="platform:StatusLineBaseType">
        <xs:sequence>
          <xs:element name="Title" type="platform:DescriptionType" minOccurs="0">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Brief title of the issue or reply.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="Due" type="DateTimeType" minOccurs="0">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>The timestamp for when the line item is a due.  For an issue, all replies are due.  For a reply, just the one reply is due.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="AssignedTo" type="ParticipantRefType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>The identifier for a participant in this RFI assigned to this issue or reply. For an issue, this is who manages the issue, assigns respondents, and issues any final reply.  For a reply, this is preparing the reply.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Multiple participants are allowed for collaborative scenarios have co-mangers of an issue or co-authors on a reply.  A collaborative scenario with multiple respondents can also be modeled with separate replies for each co-author upon which a separate, final reply is dependent.  Any partial contribution by each co-author could then be modeled separately and composed into a final reply.  How this is modeled is not prescribed by agcXML.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="DependsOn" type="RFILineRefType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>An identifier for another line item upon which this line item depends.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>When issue Q2 depends on issue Q1, issue Q2 is a sub-issue of Q1.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>When reply R2 depends on reply R1, reply R2 must take into account R1.  R2 can not complete, and is potentially blocked, until R1 completes.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Only issue-to-issue or reply-to-reply dependencies are supported.  That is, issues can not depend on replies, and replies can not depend on issues.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>The only expectation is that dependent items finish first.  There is no restriction in how dependencies are formed except that cycles are not allowed.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="DescriptionAttachment" type="platform:AttachmentType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>The statement of the issue or reply using an attached document.  Additional attachments and notes can be made to clarify.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Alternatively, the entire statement of the issue or reply can be made using free form text with field platform:Description.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Fields platform:Description and DescriptionAttachment can be used together or as alternatives.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="Stage" type="WorkflowStageType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>A workflow stage annotated with a timestamp and notes</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>The intent is for multiple stage values to represent a progression in time for the workflow.  The DateTime field provides the natural ordering and is recommended over the the sequential ordering of &lt;Stage&gt; fields.  However, agcXML does not mandate how to order stages.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="IssuedBy" type="ParticipantRefType" minOccurs="0">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Reference to the participant (person and organization) that issued this RFIIssue, RFISubIssue or RFIReply.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>For an issue, this is expected to be a general contractor, a construction manager or a prime designer such as an architect.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>For a reply, this is expected to be the author of the reply.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>In general, though, any role within a construction project can issue an RFI and its line items.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="IssuedOn" type="DateTimeType" minOccurs="0">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Timestamp for when the RFIIssue, RFISubIssue or RFIReply was issued.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="CreatedBy" type="ParticipantRefType" minOccurs="0">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>An identifier for a Participant in this RFI given credit for creating this RFIIssue, RFISubIssue or RFIReply record.  Who actually creates the record is not prescribed by agcXML.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>This participant is possibly different than the AssignedTo field.  It is generally set to a managing participant of an RFI (such as the prime contractor, the prime designer, or the construction manager) who actually manages and RFI issue or delegates work to respondents.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>The value is also used to record who suggested an RFIIssue, RFISubIssue or RFIReply when Stage=Suggested.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="CreatedOn" type="DateTimeType" minOccurs="0">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>The timestamp for when this RFIIssue, RFISubIssue or RFIReply record was created.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="ElapsedTime" type="WorkflowElapsedTimeType" minOccurs="0">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>TODO doc for WorkflowElapsedTimeType</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="ImportanceCode" type="RFIImportanceCodeType" minOccurs="0">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Importance of the RFI, issue or reply.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Enumerated values are:  Low, Medium, High.  This is an open code list in agcXML.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element ref="DisciplineCode" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>The discipline addressed in documents such as RFIs.  Example:  architectural, mechanical or structural.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Enumerated values are:  Architectural, GeneralRequirements, Mechanical, Structural, Electrical, Plumbing.  This is an open code list in agcXML.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Values in an issue reflect what is expected.  Values in a reply could serve as an override or correction.  This type of change is natural when experts prepare a reply to a loosely worded or partially understood issue.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="CategoryCode" type="RFICategoryCodeType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>The category for this RFI such as engineering conflict, insufficient information or drawing discrepancy.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Enumerated values are:  AlternateProposal, Confirmation, DrawingDiscrepancy, EngineeringConflict, FieldCondition, InsufficientInformation, PlanOrSpecDiscrepancy, SpecificationClarification.  This is an open code list in agcXML.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Values in an issue reflect what is expected.  Values in a reply could serve as an override or correction.  This type of change is natural when experts prepare a reply to a loosely worded or partially understood issue.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element ref="ImpactAssessment" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>An assessment of the impact for a change in workflow, such as RFI, on a construction project.  Examples include cost, schedule, drawing and environmental impacts.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Depending on the stage of the workflow, the impact could be expected, suggested, proposed, constrained or final.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>An impact assessment includes an impact code, narratives, attachments and supporting dtata.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>For example, a cost impact would use ImpactCode=Cost and the Amount field.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>A schedule impact would use ImpactCode=Schedule and the Duration, DateTime and Time fields as needed.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>A drawing impact would use ImpactCode=Drawing and the platform:Attachment field.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>There are other impact codes and custom impact codes are allowed.  Generally, each impact code uses the appropriate fields.  In addition, any impact assessment can include notes (including author and timestamp), free form descriptions, attachments and references.  To aid automation, numbers, dates, amounts and other data mentioned in the free form text can be included in corresponding fields.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>All field types are the general purpose "Open" variety in that they carry a "typeCode" that parties can agree upon to find very specific uses for general concepts of amounts, dates, durations, etc.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>In a reply, cost, schedule, drawing and other impacts are more definitive.  In an issue, an impact analysis is part of framing the question to be answered.  Impact analyses for an issue can be a suggestion, recommendation, constraint or best guess.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
          <xs:element name="Failure" type="platform:FailureType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <xs:annotation>
              <xs:documentation>Information about a problem, possible symptoms that may lead to the problem, and the cause and remedy for the problem.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Causes and remedies can be part of multi-level reporting. For example, a failure A can have causes P and Q as factors, and remedies X, Y and Z are to be taken.</xs:documentation>
              <xs:documentation>Failures logged here are for those attributed to this issue or reply only.  Failures across multiple issues and replies should be logged in the RFIHeader.Failure field.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
          </xs:element>
        </xs:sequence>
      </xs:extension>
    </xs:complexContent>
  </xs:complexType>
</xs:schema>